; DOMAIN: mathematics/functional-analysis/spectral-theory [CLOSED, derived 2026-08-01]; Claim: Spectral decomposition resolves linear operators on Hilbert spaces into integrals over their spectrum via projection-valued measures. For a normal operator T on a Hilbert space H, there exists a unique projection-valued measure E on the Borel sets of σ(T) such that T = ∫ λ dE(λ). This generalizes finite-dimensional diagonalization (spectral theorem) to bounded and unbounded operators, yielding Borel functional calculus f(T) = ∫ f(λ) dE(λ). For compact operators, the decomposition is a countable sum T = Σ λ_n ⟨·,e_n⟩e_n. Spectral multiplicity theory classifies operators up to unitary equivalence by their multiplicity function and measure class. Bridges to spectral_theorem (finite-dim diagonalization), operator_algebra (von Neumann algebra spectral theory), and representation_theory (decomposition into irreducibles).;; Spectral decomposition: normal operator T = integral of lambda dE(lambda) for projection-valued measure E
(|- ((==> (&& (--> HilbertSpace $H) (--> NormalOperator $T $H) (--> ProjectionValuedMeasure $E) (--> Spectrum $sigma $T))) (--> SpectralDecomposition $T (Integral $lambda $E))) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Borel functional calculus: f(T) = integral of f(lambda) dE(lambda) for bounded Borel functions f
(|- ((==> (&& (--> HilbertSpace $H) (--> NormalOperator $T $H) (--> ProjectionValuedMeasure $E) (--> BoundedBorelFunction $f))) (--> BorelFunctionalCalculus ($f $T) (Integral $f $E))) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Compact operator: T = sum of lambda_n times projection onto eigenspace e_n
(|- ((==> (&& (--> HilbertSpace $H) (--> CompactOperator $T $H) (--> Eigenvalue $lambda_n) (--> Eigenvector $e_n) (--> OrthonormalBasis $e_n))) (--> CompactSpectralDecomposition $T (Sum $lambda_n (Projection $e_n)))) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Spectral multiplicity: operator classified by multiplicity function m(lambda) and measure class on the spectrum
(|- ((==> (&& (--> NormalOperator $T $H) (--> MultiplicityFunction $m) (--> MeasureClass $mu) (--> Spectrum $sigma $T))) (--> SpectralMultiplicity $T $m $mu)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Projection-valued measure: E(A) is an orthogonal projection, E(empty)=0, E(sigma)=I, E(A intersect B)=E(A)E(B)
(|- ((==> (&& (--> HilbertSpace $H) (--> OrthogonalProjection $E_A) (--> CountablyAdditive $E) (--> Equals ($E $empty) (ZeroOperator)) (--> Equals ($E $sigma) (IdentityOperator)))) (--> ProjectionValuedMeasure $E)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Bounded spectral theorem: bounded normal operator decomposes via PVM on compact spectrum
(|- ((==> (&& (--> HilbertSpace $H) (--> BoundedOperator $T $H) (--> NormalOperator $T $H) (--> CompactSpectrum $sigma $T) (--> ProjectionValuedMeasure $E))) (--> BoundedSpectralTheorem $T $E $sigma)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Unbounded spectral theorem: self-adjoint (possibly unbounded) operator decomposes via PVM on sigma(T)
(|- ((==> (&& (--> HilbertSpace $H) (--> UnboundedOperator $T $H) (--> SelfAdjoint $T) (--> ProjectionValuedMeasure $E) (--> Spectrum $sigma $T))) (--> UnboundedSpectralTheorem $T $E $sigma)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Unitary equivalence: two normal operators are unitarily equivalent iff their spectral measures are equivalent
(|- ((==> (&& (--> NormalOperator $T1 $H1) (--> NormalOperator $T2 $H2) (--> Unitary $U) (--> Equals $T2 (Compose $U $T1 (Adjoint $U))) (--> EquivalentSpectralMeasures $E1 $E2))) (--> UnitaryEquivalence $T1 $T2)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
